<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques Callot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1592–<a href="/years/1635">1635</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques Callot was a printmaker and draughtsman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> for the Medici before returning home around 1621. He produced more than 1,400 etchings and greatly refined the technique. His series <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-miseries-and-misfortunes-of-war">The Miseries and Misfortunes of War</a>, published in 1633, depicts the brutality of soldiers during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-callot.jpg" alt="Jacques Callot" width="239" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Vorsterman (1595-1675) after Anthony van Dyck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacobus_Callot_after_A._van_Dyck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
